+
+### Exact pre-request enrollment record and crash recovery (resolves CL-P04-D01-F1)
+
+Use one closed JSON value in ONE Cloudroom secret-setting field for the durable enrollment state; do not spread the authoritative candidate across separately updated settings. Pending contains state/version, canonical approved HTTPS origin, installation UUID, preallocated journal UUID, exact frozen name/request body (including transient pairing code and the two public verifier hashes), raw claim-recovery secret and raw machine credential. Generate these once, validate all fields and await that single secret-setting write BEFORE the first enrollment/status/credential-bearing request. It is a0600 secret file and never ordinary KV/SQLite/frontend/log/URL data; non-secret identity values may be duplicated there to make the candidate recoverable. The existing SDK secret writer uses a0600 temporary file then atomic rename (secret-storage/src/secret-file.ts54–65), so a process interruption sees the prior complete state or the complete new state; no multi-file transaction is assumed. This claims process-crash/reload persistence, not a new power-loss guarantee from the SDK.
+
+The wire request remains the existing PairingClaim: code,installation_id,name,claim_verifier,credential_verifier. Verify hashes with the existing Rust producer semantics. A pending restart first uses x-openbot-pair-claim(raw original claim secret) and x-openbot-installation-id(original saved UUID) against the saved origin. Recover the same server receipt after response loss; if needed, retry only the exact saved request/claim. Never generate a replacement installation/claim/credential because of timeout, expiry or response loss. If a pairing code must be entered again, keep the ambiguous original candidate until its status is resolved; do not rebind it to another origin/company silently.
+
+Local completion order: (1) pending secret record durable before request; (2) matching receipt validated against that installation; (3) one SQLite transaction initializes/binds non-secret machine/company/installation/origin/journal metadata, using the preallocated journal UUID; existing metadata must match and is never erased/reinitialized; (4) atomically replace the one secret record with Paired, retaining origin/installation/journal/machine binding and machine credential, dropping the now-unneeded code/claim secret; (5) clear the separate transient code input, then allow WSS/SDK work. A stale input code after a crash cannot create another enrollment because the authoritative Paired/Pending state wins. No connector command/SDK mutation is admitted before the Paired promotion and bound journal agree.
+
+Crash cuts: before pending write, no network was allowed; after pending write/before request, restart reuses exact candidate; after server commit/lost response or after receipt/before SQLite completion, status recovery returns the same receipt and completes the same binding; after SQLite commit/before Paired promotion, Pending + matching existing journal completes promotion without resetting any rows; after Paired promotion/before input cleanup, Paired + matching journal resumes and clears stale input only. Paired + missing/mismatched journal is recovery-required and cannot create a new journal, even though its expected UUID is present in the secret. Missing/corrupt secret state with existing metadata also fails closed, never fabricates a fresh candidate. Unknown/corrupt state cannot fall through to initial pairing. A deliberate repair flow is separate from automatic recovery and must retain old thread/journal evidence.
+
+Required assertions reopen actual persisted secret/journal state at EACH cut, including interruption between every separate write, lost server response, stale code input, origin change and post-pair journal deletion. Assert same installation/claim/request/credential/origin/machine, no second server enrollment and no SDK call during incomplete enrollment. These are actual crash/reopen checks, not memory-only retry assertions.
 
 Bind credentials to the approved canonical HTTPS origin at enrollment; changing origin while paired fails configuration instead of sending the old credential elsewhere. Reject userinfo/path/query/fragment/non-HTTPS and redirects on pairing; normal certificate validation stays on, WSS tests use trusted test CA. Credential only in Authorization header. Authorization rejection stops reconnect retry until explicit pairing repair/settings change. Ordinary network loss retains all identity/journal/native state.
 
